<article class="env-definition" id="0231">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0231">Definition <span data-tag="0231">39.13.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A <em>groupoid scheme over $S$</em>, or simply a <em>groupoid over $S$</em> is a quintuple $(U, R, s, t, c)$ where $U$ and $R$ are schemes over $S$, and $s, t : R \to U$ and $c : R \times _{s, U, t} R \to R$ are morphisms of schemes over $S$ with the following property: For any scheme $T$ over $S$ the quintuple 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  (U(T), R(T), s, t, c)  \]
</div>
<p>
 is a groupoid category in the sense described above. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>morphism $f : (U, R, s, t, c) \to (U', R', s', t', c')$ of groupoid schemes over $S$</em> is given by morphisms of schemes $f : U \to U'$ and $f : R \to R'$ with the following property: For any scheme $T$ over $S$ the maps $f$ define a functor from the groupoid category $(U(T), R(T), s, t, c)$ to the groupoid category $(U'(T), R'(T), s', t', c')$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>